cff-version: 1.2.0
message: >-
  If you use jbubble in your research, cite it with the metadata in this file.
  jbubble was first presented at the 2026 IEEE International Ultrasonics
  Symposium (IUS).
type: software
title: "jbubble: differentiable microbubble dynamics in JAX"
abstract: >-
  jbubble is a JAX library for simulating and fitting the radial dynamics of
  acoustically driven microbubbles, such as ultrasound contrast agents. It
  composes equations of motion (Rayleigh-Plesset, modified Rayleigh-Plesset,
  Keller-Miksis, and Gilmore) with gas, shell, surface tension, and medium
  models, and with driving pulses that range from tone bursts to measured or
  neural waveforms. Every model is a PyTree, so simulations compile with
  jax.jit, batch with jax.vmap and a parallel grid sweep, and differentiate
  with jax.grad through an adaptive ODE solver. jbubble uses these gradients
  to fit model parameters and neural shell laws to measured radius curves or
  acoustic emissions, and computes the pressure that a bubble radiates.
# Other contributors are listed once the maintainer confirms them.
authors:
  - given-names: Callum Rhys
    family-names: Tilbury
    affiliation: Imperial College London
version: 0.2.0
license: MIT
repository-code: "https://github.com/imperial-nsb/jbubble"
url: "https://imperial-nsb.github.io/jbubble/"
keywords:
  - jax
  - microbubbles
  - ultrasound
  - contrast agents
  - cavitation
  - differentiable simulation
  - acoustic emission
  - parameter estimation
